1
00:00:02,900 --> 00:00:07,780
give a dare about the company and sometimes they don't know what the company makes there's only

2
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:11,860
one way you're going to get them interested in the company and that's not by telling them how

3
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:18,740
interesting you are and having the picture of the ceo on the annual report uh you've got to make it

4
00:00:18,740 --> 00:00:24,790
financially worth their while and i came up with a very simple suggestion which was for differential

5
00:00:24,790 --> 00:00:31,270
dividends depending on how long you have owned the stock uh actually i said stock dividends

6
00:00:31,270 --> 00:00:35,830
differential stock dividends for every year or two years or three years or whatever for every

7
00:00:35,830 --> 00:00:41,430
period of time that you own the stock you get an a stock dividend which those who have owned

8
00:00:41,430 --> 00:00:48,090
the stock for only shorter periods of time do not get a great deal of interest in this proposal

9
00:00:48,090 --> 00:00:54,970
because it does make sense i mean over a period of years you build up people who if they sit with

10
00:00:54,970 --> 00:01:01,620
the stock to begin with are pretty much protected against loss that is if they are the recipients

11
00:01:01,620 --> 00:01:03,800
of differential stock dividends,

12
00:01:04,220 --> 00:01:05,440
advantageous stock dividends.

13
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:07,200
Over a period of 10 years,

14
00:01:07,460 --> 00:01:09,240
in effect, their purchase price

15
00:01:09,240 --> 00:01:11,800
has been reduced by 30%, 40%,

16
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:15,180
even regardless of the cash dividends they receive.

17
00:01:15,700 --> 00:01:17,640
And that pretty much protects them

18
00:01:17,640 --> 00:01:22,830
against any decline in the price of the stock,

19
00:01:22,990 --> 00:01:24,350
and that means they're never going to be

20
00:01:24,350 --> 00:01:25,930
wildly unhappy with you.

21
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:27,590
And of course, if the stock goes up,

22
00:01:27,650 --> 00:01:29,050
they're going to be very, very happy indeed.

23
00:01:29,050 --> 00:01:33,800
So the analytical basis is very clear.

24
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:42,500
And I spoke to the heads of several of the largest corporations in the company on this matter,

25
00:01:42,540 --> 00:01:45,320
and they said, that's a very interesting idea.

26
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:47,060
Let's see if we can do it.

27
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:57,610
And they put their lawyers on it naturally, and naturally, with what results you know.

28
00:01:59,930 --> 00:02:03,150
Whenever a corporate CEO doesn't want to do anything,

29
00:02:03,150 --> 00:02:12,060
he—whenever he wants to cave in or not do anything, he consults counsel.

30
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:18,060
And I began to receive all sorts of learned documents explaining why this was very difficult

31
00:02:18,060 --> 00:02:22,280
under SEC regulations and et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

32
00:02:22,280 --> 00:02:26,260
And finally, at one point, going on for nine years, I had really—I had told the idea

33
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:29,220
to them, thinking, it's there, baby, let them run with it.

34
00:02:29,220 --> 00:02:34,180
I'm not going to launch a campaign for a stockholder constituency.

35
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:38,740
And at one point, only about a year ago, I got terribly irritated at the fact that nine

36
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:41,610
years had passed and nothing had happened.

37
00:02:41,610 --> 00:02:45,290
And so I wrote to Harold Williams, and I said, I want an official opinion.

38
00:02:45,290 --> 00:02:46,290
I know Harold.

39
00:02:46,290 --> 00:02:49,070
I said, I want an official opinion from you.

40
00:02:49,070 --> 00:02:52,950
Is there an SEC problem in this or not?

41
00:02:52,950 --> 00:02:56,410
And Harold Williams sent it around to his staff, and he sent me a very legalistic document.

42
00:02:56,410 --> 00:03:01,730
Turns out there's no SEC problem at all with differential stock dividends.

43
00:03:01,730 --> 00:03:10,300
There may be problems at the state level with state legislation.

44
00:03:10,300 --> 00:03:14,540
If there are such problems, and even then, no one seems to be quite sure, but they're

45
00:03:14,540 --> 00:03:15,540
easily solved.

46
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:20,960
I mean, what state legislature is not going to benefit investors at the expense of speculators?

47
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:22,940
It would be, I think, a very popular move.

48
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:28,390
Why shouldn't they let corporations do that if corporations want to do it?

49
00:03:28,390 --> 00:03:33,850
The sad truth, and I have come to this conclusion most reluctantly, is that the reason it isn't

50
00:03:33,850 --> 00:03:38,730
happening is not because it's difficult, not because there are legal hurdles, because the

51
00:03:38,730 --> 00:03:43,690
last thing in the world that CEOs want is an active, concerned, interested stockholder

52
00:03:43,690 --> 00:03:51,890
constituency. I think, I suppose, a CEO, every night before he goes to sleep, has many nightmares.

53
00:03:52,050 --> 00:03:55,310
One of them would be to wake up in the morning and find his office full of stockholders.

54
00:03:57,450 --> 00:04:05,840
And this is so, and it's understandable. It's like a professor waking up in the morning and

55
00:04:05,840 --> 00:04:12,640
findings house full of students. I mean, we have those nightmares too. But in the end, if you take

56
00:04:12,640 --> 00:04:21,380
seriously your role as a part of a political faction or as a political faction contending

57
00:04:21,380 --> 00:04:27,980
with other political factions, you have to take seriously the responsibility of organizing your

58
00:04:27,980 --> 00:04:36,550
potential strength. And it seems to me sad and negligent that corporations should have failed

59
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:45,230
to explore this idea more seriously than they have, because I do think that over a period of

60
00:04:45,230 --> 00:04:55,430
time, it would be very, very useful to them. I also think there's so many other things that

61
00:04:55,430 --> 00:05:03,500
corporations can and should do. I don't think, and here I disagree, I have to say I really

62
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:08,180
disagree with Peter. I don't want to see American corporations sit down with Peter Drucker or with

63
00:05:08,180 --> 00:05:12,400
me or anybody and say, don't tell us what's good for the corporation. Tell us what we should do

64
00:05:12,400 --> 00:05:29,440
that's good for the country. Please. And business is for business. Businessmen have a basic trained

65
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:37,260
incapacity to understand politics well, because in business, the whole mode of business is

66
00:05:37,260 --> 00:05:47,480
different from the mode of politics. Business is a decision-making activity, practical activity

67
00:05:47,480 --> 00:05:51,860
of a decision-making kind. It doesn't matter whether decision-making is hierarchical or

68
00:05:51,860 --> 00:05:55,700
consensual, but it's a decision-making activity. Politics is not a decision-making

69
00:05:55,700 --> 00:06:00,800
activity. It's an activity in which you rarely get the opportunity or the pleasure of making

70
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:05,620
a decision. Most of the time, it is a negotiating activity, which is, incidentally, why lawyers tend

71
00:06:05,620 --> 00:06:11,300
to be quite good at it and have played such a prominent role in modern democratic politics.

72
00:06:11,300 --> 00:06:21,580
I think that corporations coping with this new environment and this environment that will

73
00:06:21,580 --> 00:06:25,520
persist for quite a while, really have to think

74
00:06:25,520 --> 00:06:30,590
of changing, of not only trying to secure a

75
00:06:30,590 --> 00:06:35,840
solid base with a vested interest

76
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:39,500
in the corporations, and I don't think the pension funds will provide that

77
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:44,420
because the pension funds involved in the corporation is consistent

78
00:06:44,420 --> 00:06:48,260
with any kind of management of the corporation, and it's

79
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:52,060
consistent with socialized corporations, with Ralph Nades corporations

80
00:06:52,060 --> 00:06:53,460
as well as the ones that exist.

81
00:06:54,080 --> 00:06:56,780
And I really am most interested in a corporation

82
00:06:56,780 --> 00:07:00,740
as a bulwark of a free society.

83
00:07:01,500 --> 00:07:05,100
Even the least admirable corporations are that,

84
00:07:05,180 --> 00:07:09,040
just as I agree, I think the proliferation of churches

85
00:07:09,040 --> 00:07:11,540
and religious institutions in this society

86
00:07:11,540 --> 00:07:12,780
are bulwarks of a free society.

87
00:07:12,820 --> 00:07:14,320
I don't agree with all of them, obviously.

88
00:07:14,540 --> 00:07:15,300
I only agree with one.

89
00:07:17,320 --> 00:07:19,060
And I don't even like some of them.

90
00:07:19,920 --> 00:07:20,580
But never mind.

91
00:07:21,730 --> 00:07:23,690
The fact that they're there is very important.

92
00:07:23,870 --> 00:07:29,150
And the fact that they exist as independent centers of power is very important.

93
00:07:29,250 --> 00:07:36,200
Therefore, I think it's very important that corporations continue to exist as independent sources of power.

94
00:07:36,760 --> 00:07:39,940
For this to happen, however, vested interests will have to be created.

95
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:42,660
I think corporate leadership is going to be attentive to anything.

96
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:44,480
I think it should be more attentive to small business.

97
00:07:45,780 --> 00:07:51,640
Without the buttressing of small business, small business has the political strength.

98
00:07:52,160 --> 00:07:54,700
You want to get something through Congress, don't go to the top CEOs.

99
00:07:55,060 --> 00:08:00,660
Go to those local undertakers, those local druggists, those local, you forgot one very important small business group,

100
00:08:00,740 --> 00:08:02,000
those local automobile dealers.

101
00:08:02,340 --> 00:08:03,280
Not this year, perhaps.

102
00:08:03,880 --> 00:08:11,220
But the richest man in town is usually the local automobile dealer, not the plant manager of a major factory.

103
00:08:12,830 --> 00:08:14,450
These are the people with influence.

104
00:08:14,450 --> 00:08:20,690
These are the people who are the business establishment in local communities, and they are the important ones.

105
00:08:20,690 --> 00:08:28,560
If business leaders find that difficult to do, corporate leaders, then go for a stockholder constituency.

106
00:08:28,920 --> 00:08:36,380
In addition, of course, there is the whole question of how you communicate with other factions in society

107
00:08:36,380 --> 00:08:40,700
or with other potential allies or potential adversaries.

108
00:08:41,020 --> 00:08:44,640
I really think business communications are an absolutely terrible state,

109
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:53,360
largely because they have been handled mainly by personal relations, which are out of date, as Peter says,

110
00:08:53,420 --> 00:08:56,480
or by public relations, which is not the name of the game today.

111
00:08:57,600 --> 00:09:02,200
Public relations is an activity whereby you try to persuade journalists to think well of you.

112
00:09:04,240 --> 00:09:07,480
Not a very productive activity at the moment.

113
00:09:09,660 --> 00:09:14,320
And it was only a productive activity when journalists didn't care what they wrote about business.

114
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:19,340
It's only a productive activity when journalists and publishers were bribed by business, to put it very bluntly.

115
00:09:21,720 --> 00:09:28,520
The corporation has to have a sense of its communications with its employees.

116
00:09:30,380 --> 00:09:33,040
Corporations don't share problems with employees.

117
00:09:33,640 --> 00:09:34,180
Very interesting.

118
00:09:34,360 --> 00:09:38,540
They boast of their accomplishment, but corporate leadership, corporate management,

119
00:09:38,700 --> 00:09:42,960
hates sharing problems with anyone, including, of course, stockholders.

120
00:09:43,680 --> 00:09:44,180
It's interesting.

121
00:09:44,500 --> 00:09:46,940
I've been interested in the automobile situation.

122
00:09:48,090 --> 00:09:57,000
Now, American automobile companies really have been disadvantaged by government regulations vis-a-vis the foreign imports.

123
00:09:57,080 --> 00:10:02,290
There's just no question about that, all sorts of government regulations.

124
00:10:04,250 --> 00:10:12,670
But at no point that I have been able to determine has the management of corporations been going down to their workers and saying,

125
00:10:12,670 --> 00:10:17,590
look, this is the problem, and explaining in great detail why we're not doing well.

126
00:10:17,590 --> 00:10:25,290
I don't believe Chrysler ever did that to its employees, explaining why we're having the problems we're having.

127
00:10:25,430 --> 00:10:28,070
I don't expect them to confess their sins of lousy management.

128
00:10:28,210 --> 00:10:29,370
That would be too much to expect.

129
00:10:29,370 --> 00:10:38,370
But at least to explain why the government regulations were having that particular impact upon Chrysler.

130
00:10:39,070 --> 00:10:42,410
I don't believe there was any effort to communicate to employees in that way.

131
00:10:42,810 --> 00:10:45,130
Corporate leaders don't like communicating problems,

132
00:10:45,130 --> 00:10:48,850
but communicating problems are what really is interesting.

133
00:10:48,850 --> 00:10:51,030
No one's interested in just success stories.

134
00:10:51,030 --> 00:10:53,530
Problems, particularly if they're gonna affect your life

135
00:10:53,530 --> 00:10:57,510
and your job and your career, are very interesting indeed.

136
00:10:57,510 --> 00:10:59,750
I mean, I don't understand why drug companies

137
00:10:59,750 --> 00:11:02,720
aren't explaining to their employees

138
00:11:02,720 --> 00:11:03,660
some of the problems they have

139
00:11:03,660 --> 00:11:06,020
with the Food and Drug Administration.

140
00:11:06,020 --> 00:11:08,020
They shouldn't, people should know this.

141
00:11:09,420 --> 00:11:11,020
And similarly with every industry,

142
00:11:11,020 --> 00:11:12,780
all of which has enormous problems

143
00:11:12,780 --> 00:11:14,720
with foreign competition perhaps,

144
00:11:14,720 --> 00:11:18,340
but always with government, no effort is made to,

145
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:20,520
I won't even use the word educate,

146
00:11:20,520 --> 00:11:23,540
but just simply to inform their employees

147
00:11:23,540 --> 00:11:26,640
in a continuous way about the problems

148
00:11:26,640 --> 00:11:27,900
the business is facing.

149
00:11:27,900 --> 00:11:30,960
So suddenly, when things go bad,

150
00:11:30,960 --> 00:11:34,640
everyone is not surprised, and being surprised

151
00:11:34,640 --> 00:11:39,090
becomes very suspicious of what is going on.

152
00:11:39,090 --> 00:11:41,450
No point in communicating this with the union leadership.

153
00:11:41,450 --> 00:11:44,740
You want to communicate it with the employees.

154
00:11:44,740 --> 00:12:01,750
And the last point I wish to make involves the relationship of these corporate leaders who, as I agree, are here to stay, heads of major organizations, with the most powerful community in our society, namely the intellectual and academic community.

155
00:12:01,750 --> 00:12:27,110
The situation is improving, but very, very slowly, in part because even today your average CEO doesn't understand how important the intellectual and academic communities are, though I have begun to get through to them now by saying, look, what do you have vis-a-vis the academic community?

156
00:12:27,110 --> 00:12:27,730
You have money.

157
00:12:28,720 --> 00:12:32,260
You can ring up a senator, and he'll probably return your call.

158
00:12:32,340 --> 00:12:34,160
You have influence of that kind.

159
00:12:35,970 --> 00:12:37,350
But they have hostages.

160
00:12:37,690 --> 00:12:39,510
They've got your children as hostages.

161
00:12:40,450 --> 00:12:42,230
You know, when we have a situation today,

162
00:12:42,290 --> 00:12:47,330
when I would wager half the children of the chief executive's offices of this country

163
00:12:47,330 --> 00:12:52,680
despise business or have a low opinion of business as an activity,

164
00:12:52,680 --> 00:12:56,480
you begin to wonder, how in God's name is business going to communicate?

165
00:12:57,040 --> 00:12:59,840
How is it going to successfully defend the business system?

166
00:13:00,280 --> 00:13:03,880
And the answer is that in a war between the business and academic communities,

167
00:13:04,980 --> 00:13:06,920
the academic community will always win.

168
00:13:07,540 --> 00:13:09,220
It may lose a temporary battle.

169
00:13:09,520 --> 00:13:13,620
It'll always win the war in the long run because it has the children.

170
00:13:14,360 --> 00:13:17,440
They own the children, yours and mine.

171
00:13:18,770 --> 00:13:23,170
And so this gulf that exists between the academic intellectual community on the one hand

172
00:13:23,170 --> 00:13:25,750
and the business community on the other is tragic.

173
00:13:25,750 --> 00:13:31,250
Efforts must be made to bridge it on both sides.

174
00:13:31,250 --> 00:13:36,850
I have no great illusions as to how easy it's going to be, but it's very difficult.

175
00:13:36,850 --> 00:13:38,590
And you run into all sorts of difficulties.

176
00:13:38,590 --> 00:13:42,940
You run into the difficulty that business executives are too busy to read, and being

177
00:13:42,940 --> 00:13:48,170
too busy to read, their intellectual muscles atrophy.

178
00:13:48,170 --> 00:13:52,130
Not their analytical muscles, they're still perfectly good, competent at analyzing a problem

179
00:13:52,130 --> 00:13:56,770
very cogently, but their intellectual muscles, which are not the same thing as analytical

180
00:13:56,770 --> 00:14:03,080
muscles, intellectual muscles being the muscle which permits you to play with an idea, to

181
00:14:03,080 --> 00:14:08,560
entertain it, speculate about it, think about it, not necessarily come to a conclusion about

182
00:14:08,560 --> 00:14:12,080
it right away, not in a decision-making context.

183
00:14:14,260 --> 00:14:18,680
I don't really know what to do about this, except that I do think that larger corporations

184
00:14:18,680 --> 00:14:27,640
are going to have to somehow internalize their knowledge, the attitudes, not necessarily

185
00:14:27,640 --> 00:14:31,380
the attitudes, the knowledge and expertise of the academic and intellectual milieu.

186
00:14:31,520 --> 00:14:51,700
They really need, they've got to somehow learn what's happening in that world, learn to appreciate in that world, have people fairly high up who read it, who read what's written in that world. I'll take a test right now. I assume the majority of you are business executives.

187
00:14:53,200 --> 00:14:58,040
How many of you subscribe to the New Republic?

188
00:14:58,040 --> 00:15:00,440
How many of you subscribe to Commentary?

189
00:15:02,140 --> 00:15:04,480
You don't know what's happening in the world.

190
00:15:04,480 --> 00:15:06,750
It's true, you really don't.

191
00:15:06,750 --> 00:15:08,990
That's where the future is being shaped.

192
00:15:08,990 --> 00:15:11,780
It's where your future is being shaped,

193
00:15:11,780 --> 00:15:13,680
and not necessarily in those two magazines,

194
00:15:13,680 --> 00:15:15,580
though I think I picked the two best,

195
00:15:15,580 --> 00:15:17,320
the one slightly right of center,

196
00:15:17,320 --> 00:15:18,720
one slightly left of center,

197
00:15:19,720 --> 00:15:21,820
but in magazines of that kind.

198
00:15:21,820 --> 00:15:25,260
Somehow or other, the corporation has to

199
00:15:25,260 --> 00:15:30,900
have links with that other world of action,

200
00:15:31,220 --> 00:15:36,340
the world of ideas where your future and mine, I guess, will be shaped.

201
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:39,860
Well, I could go on indefinitely, but it's time for the break.

202
00:15:39,860 --> 00:15:42,520
And I can't go on indefinitely. I could go on for a while.

203
00:15:44,540 --> 00:15:45,960
But it's time for the break.

204
00:15:45,980 --> 00:15:49,420
In any case, we will have further occasion in the question period

205
00:15:49,420 --> 00:15:51,320
to expand on any of these points.

206
00:15:52,340 --> 00:17:06,520
I read some of these calls and to talk about movies and so on.

207
00:17:06,520 --> 00:17:14,200
And let me go back to something Professor Kristol said, which one cannot emphasize enough

208
00:17:14,200 --> 00:17:22,060
when he said that he's an old enough political scientist to know that one has to build on interest

209
00:17:22,060 --> 00:17:26,940
and cannot build on sentiment.

210
00:17:28,170 --> 00:17:32,250
And public relations people do not understand that, bluntly.

211
00:17:32,850 --> 00:17:37,650
And interests have to be overt, visible, and institutionalized

212
00:17:37,650 --> 00:18:08,040
to be effective. This is a lesson as old as Aristotle. If I sound old-fashioned, this is because the lesson is. And how do we institutionalize it? What, to my mind, is lacking in the campaigns today and why the impact is, I think, they are rhetorical successes, is that there is no follow-up.

213
00:18:08,040 --> 00:18:30,640
And that has to be institutionalized in an interest. And how, I don't know whether that is through inside the business. And by the way, when Earth, Crystal and I say large company, we mean different things because he's looking at the Fortune 500 or Fortune 1000.

214
00:18:30,640 --> 00:18:41,620
and I think that in terms of its behavior, its structure, the company he thinks is small business,

215
00:18:41,820 --> 00:18:47,580
a couple of hundred employees and local, really is part of the small business world

216
00:18:47,580 --> 00:18:54,500
and not of the large business world and not the cigar store owner or the carpenter's world.

217
00:18:55,440 --> 00:19:03,500
How does one institutionalize the interest so that it's overt?

218
00:19:03,900 --> 00:19:11,900
and that it is real and not just an appeal to rhetoric. I think that is a great problem.

219
00:19:11,900 --> 00:19:17,820
Somebody here in the course asked what could Chrysler do? Well, now it's a little late, you know.

220
00:19:21,600 --> 00:19:28,160
But what could Chrysler have done? That is a great question. And they didn't do anything.

221
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:43,520
and here is the steel industry and it's again very late and in many ways of

222
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:51,880
course the large steel plant is technologically on the verge of being

223
00:19:51,880 --> 00:19:58,360
obsolete and we have a serious recession we could probably the world could get by

224
00:19:58,360 --> 00:20:05,500
for 20 years with many plants using scrap because we have produced enough

225
00:20:05,500 --> 00:20:12,040
steel since 1945 not to have to mine any iron ore if there is any drop in steel

